### 1. Modify compiler.c – Add a Hash Table

Declare a hash table that stores identifier constants.

```c
static Table stringConstants;
```

The table maps each identifier to its index in the
constant table.

Since this uses the existing Table type, compiler.c
also needs access to its declaration through table.h.

### 2. Initialize and Free the Table

In the compile() function, initialize the table before
parsing and free it after compilation finishes.

```c
bool compile(const char* source, Chunk* chunk) {
  initScanner(source);

  compilingChunk = chunk;
  parser.hadError = false;
  parser.panicMode = false;

  initTable(&stringConstants);

  advance();

  while (!match(TOKEN_EOF)) {
    declaration();
  }

  endCompiler();

  freeTable(&stringConstants);

  return !parser.hadError;
}
```

This ensures each compilation starts with an empty
lookup table and releases its allocated table storage
when compilation is finished.

### 3. Update identifierConstant()

Before adding an identifier to the constant table, look
for it in the hash table.

```c
static uint8_t identifierConstant(Token* name) {
  ObjString* string =
      copyString(name->start, name->length);

  Value indexValue;

  if (tableGet(&stringConstants, string, &indexValue)) {
    return (uint8_t)AS_NUMBER(indexValue);
  }

  uint8_t index = makeConstant(OBJ_VAL(string));

  tableSet(&stringConstants, string,
           NUMBER_VAL((double)index));

  return index;
}
```

If the identifier already exists, its stored index is
returned instead of creating another constant.

Otherwise, the compiler adds the identifier to the
constant table and records its new index in the
hash table.

This works with clox's string interning because
identical identifier strings share the same ObjString
pointer.

## Compiler Performance

The optimization adds some work during compilation
because the compiler must perform a hash table lookup
whenever it encounters an identifier.

However, hash table lookups are approximately constant
time on average.

A simple alternative would be to scan the constant table
for duplicates, but repeated linear searches could lead
to quadratic compilation time as the number of
identifiers grows.

Using a hash table avoids that problem.

## Runtime Performance

The runtime still accesses identifier names through the
constant table, so this optimization does not directly
make global variable lookups faster.

Its main benefit is reducing duplicate constant entries
and using fewer constant table slots.

This is especially helpful because clox currently
supports only 256 constants per chunk.

